From c83d5dbb14aa2c5ccbf154449095d6b2651f947f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Aamir <aamir@tallow-lang.org>
Date: Thu, 15 Oct 2026 01:34:37 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 12/27] extract: gate the tier's third-party transfer on the
 plan's own compliance block
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The tier sends a sample of third-party page content and a prompt to whatever
engine is configured. For a loopback engine nothing leaves the box. For a remote
engine the content leaves the box to a vendor — and that transfer is exactly what
the request plan already governs through its per-request `compliance` block
(`pii_policy`, `requires_approval`), so it is governed here too instead of being
the one path that bypasses its own governance.

Fail-closed: `TransferPolicy::none()` — the default — REFUSES a remote transfer.
The reading is deliberate: "nothing said otherwise" is precisely the condition
this gate exists to refuse, not a licence to send.

A redact-class `pii_policy` is refused rather than honoured by redacting the
sample, because the tier sends the sample it was given and silently transforming
it would make the extraction's provenance describe something other than what left
the box. Refusing keeps "what the plan said" and "what was sent" the same claim.

Wiring: the policy rides the extractor (`with_transfer`), so it is enforced at the
one place that decides to call an engine rather than at each call site. The plan
supplies it via `TransferPolicy::from_compliance`; a run with no plan declares it
through TALLOW_LLM_PII_POLICY, and the source is recorded so a reader can tell
which authorised the transfer. No plan-driven caller reaches the tier today, which
is why the gate sits on the tier and not in a helper with no caller.

The transfer decision is part of `tier_state`, not a separate concern: an engine
that is reachable but not permitted to receive content is NOT enabled, and
`transfer_allowed` is in the run-evidence record alongside the rest.

Verified: cargo check --lib --bins RC=0; llm_transfer 7 passed/0 — loopback needs
no authorisation; no policy refuses; redact-class refuses; requires_approval
refuses; a declared no-PII policy permits; the specimen's real block parses; and
from_env reports "none" when it declares nothing. That last test earned its place:
the first run caught `from_env` reporting its source as the environment when no
variable was set — a gate misreporting its own provenance, which is the one thing
this module must never do. engine_tests, smart_extract and llm_extract suites pass.
